WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.872
A test fails on your pull request.

2
00:00:04.872 --> 00:00:07.492
There's the red cross, right on the page.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.524
So, can it still be merged?

4
00:00:09.524 --> 00:00:12.633
Most of us would say: of course not, it's red.

5
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:13.781
Keep your answer.

6
00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:15.471
We'll test it at the end.

7
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.760
Picture our kitchen on its first Saturday with a real taster at the pass.

8
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.204
She is careful, and that is the problem.

9
00:00:23.433 --> 00:00:26.491
Plates pile up, while every dish waits its turn.

10
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.360
Each morning the stock is boiled again from bones, because the fridge is emptied every night.

11
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:36.920
Then a waiter in a hurry carries one plate out before the taster reaches it.

12
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.727
Of course, that's the plate with the wrong sauce.

13
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:41.854
It's easy to blame the waiter.

14
00:00:41.854 --> 00:00:46.721
But a check that is slow gets skipped, and so does a check that is optional.

15
00:00:46.721 --> 00:00:48.194
People aren't careless.

16
00:00:48.194 --> 00:00:49.027
They're busy.

17
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.957
Three troubles: slow, forgetful, optional.

18
00:00:51.957 --> 00:00:55.135
Let's fix them one at a time, starting with slow.

19
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.917
A good pass doesn't taste every plate first.

20
00:00:58.917 --> 00:00:59.630
It glances.

21
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.898
Right table, right dish, still hot?

22
00:01:01.898 --> 00:01:05.267
Only plates that pass the glance get the slow taste.

23
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.956
So the lab's pipeline starts with lint.

24
00:01:07.956 --> 00:01:13.191
Ruff reads your code without running it, and flags style problems and likely bugs.

25
00:01:13.191 --> 00:01:14.276
It takes seconds.

26
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.640
At the same moment, the tests run with pytest.

27
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:21.873
Jobs run in parallel by default, each on its own fresh runner.

28
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.409
And with a matrix, one job written once runs twice, once per Python version.

29
00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:29.994
Like testing one recipe in two ovens.

30
00:01:29.994 --> 00:01:32.509
Two checks, almost no extra waiting.

31
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:36.609
Building the Docker image is the slowest check, so it waits.

32
00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:41.131
needs: [lint, test] means build starts only after all of them succeed.

33
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.203
If lint fails, build is skipped.

34
00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:47.469
You hear about the failure in seconds, without waiting for the slow part.

35
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.733
Remember that skipped box.

36
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:50.828
It comes back.

37
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:53.329
Trouble two, forgetful.

38
00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:59.433
Every job starts on an empty machine, so pip downloads every package again on every run.

39
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:01.097
Stock boiled from bones.

40
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.779
A cache is the fridge.

41
00:02:02.779 --> 00:02:08.695
It saves files from one run, such as downloaded packages, and restores them in the next.

42
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.232
Each entry is stored under a cache key, a label for what's inside.

43
00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:16.645
A good fridge label says which recipe and which day.

44
00:02:16.645 --> 00:02:19.992
This one says: the system, the Python version, pip.

45
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:26.671
And a hash, a short fingerprint computed from your requirements files, the lists of packages to install.

46
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.207
Next run, same files, same key.

47
00:02:29.207 --> 00:02:32.035
The log says: Cache restored from key.

48
00:02:32.035 --> 00:02:33.597
Seconds, not minutes.

49
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.981
Now change one letter in a requirements file.

50
00:02:36.981 --> 00:02:38.112
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:38.112 --> 00:02:39.879
Same label, or a new one?

52
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:42.811
A new one.

53
00:02:42.811 --> 00:02:45.444
The hash changes, so the key changes.

54
00:02:45.444 --> 00:02:51.704
GitHub restores an older entry with the same start, and pip downloads only what changed.

55
00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.052
Then the job saves a fresh entry.

56
00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:55.686
One rule for fridges.

57
00:02:55.686 --> 00:02:58.996
GitHub deletes caches nobody used for seven days.

58
00:02:58.996 --> 00:03:01.767
So a cache is a speed-up that can vanish.

59
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.875
Never rely on it, and never put secrets in it.

60
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.385
Trouble three, optional.

61
00:03:07.385 --> 00:03:09.036
Our pipeline is fast now.

62
00:03:09.036 --> 00:03:12.668
But a red check still stops nobody from clicking merge.

63
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:21.675
On GitHub, the fix is a ruleset on main: a named list of rules, such as: these checks must pass before a pull request can merge.

64
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.792
Here's a tempting shortcut.

65
00:03:23.792 --> 00:03:27.715
Build waits for everything, so let's require only build.

66
00:03:27.715 --> 00:03:30.168
If build passed, everything passed.

67
00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:30.588
Right?

68
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.009
Now a test fails.

69
00:03:32.009 --> 00:03:35.208
Pause and guess: can this pull request merge?

70
00:03:37.433 --> 00:03:40.305
Build needs the tests, so build is skipped.

71
00:03:40.305 --> 00:03:41.841
That skipped box again.

72
00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:46.467
And a job skipped because a job it needs failed counts as passed.

73
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:48.430
The ruleset sees build: fine.

74
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.257
The merge button lights up.

75
00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:50.806
Well.

76
00:03:50.806 --> 00:03:54.815
The waiter just carried out the plate with the wrong sauce.

77
00:03:54.815 --> 00:03:56.174
Nobody broke a rule.

78
00:03:56.174 --> 00:03:57.533
The rule had a hole.

79
00:03:57.767 --> 00:04:01.296
The fix: require every check by its exact name.

80
00:04:01.296 --> 00:04:04.150
lint, test 3.13, test 3.14, and build.

81
00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:08.581
Now the failed test itself is required, and red means stop.

82
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.923
Each of those is called a required status check.

83
00:04:11.923 --> 00:04:13.876
Two more details catch people.

84
00:04:13.876 --> 00:04:17.389
A new ruleset starts as Disabled, so set it to Active.

85
00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:22.075
And on the Free plan, rulesets are only enforced on public repositories.

86
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.073
Last guardrail.

87
00:04:24.073 --> 00:04:31.867
A slow pipeline brings back trouble one: people stop waiting, or pile many changes into one big pull request.

88
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:36.830
Concurrency with cancel-in-progress cancels a stale run when you push again.

89
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.689
The table changed its order, so you stop cooking the old dish.

90
00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.339
timeout-minutes stops a hung job early.

91
00:04:43.567 --> 00:04:48.122
And never put a path filter on pull_request when its checks are required.

92
00:04:48.122 --> 00:04:54.737
A docs-only pull request never runs the workflow, so its checks stay Pending forever, and block the merge.

93
00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:56.605
So, the moral.

94
00:04:56.605 --> 00:04:59.620
Slow gets skipped, so put quick checks first.

95
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.635
Forgetful costs minutes, so give it a fridge.

96
00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:06.253
Optional gets skipped, so require every check by name.

97
00:05:06.467 --> 00:05:08.423
And the question from the start?

98
00:05:08.423 --> 00:05:12.091
A red pull request can merge, if your rule has a hole in it.

99
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.166
Quick check.

100
00:05:13.166 --> 00:05:18.724
Next year you add Python 3.15 to the matrix, and forget to touch the ruleset.

101
00:05:18.724 --> 00:05:20.601
Is the new check required?

102
00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:21.756
Pause and think.

103
00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:24.176
No.

104
00:05:24.176 --> 00:05:27.316
The ruleset only knows the names you gave it.

105
00:05:27.316 --> 00:05:30.387
A new check stays optional until you add it.
